Discovered while fixing ttrb-fsx9.

API_BASE in app/javascript/lib/api.ts is the relative '/api/v1'. happy-dom resolves relative URLs against http://localhost:3000, so any vitest test that renders a component firing an unmocked fetch reaches for a real server. ttrb-fsx9 fixed the two files that did this today (EntryContent, SettingsDialog), but nothing stops the next one.

Proposal: override global.fetch in test/setup.ts so an unmocked call never leaves the process.

Verified during ttrb-fsx9: adding

  global.fetch = ((input: RequestInfo | URL) => {
    const url = typeof input === 'string' ? input : String(input);
    throw new Error(`Unmocked fetch to ${url}. Mock @/lib/api (or global.fetch) in this test.`);
  }) as typeof fetch;

keeps the full suite green (27 files / 522 tests) and does stop the real request: reverting EntryContent.test.tsx to its pre-fix state with the guard in place produced 27 passing tests and zero ECONNREFUSED output.

Caveat that needs deciding before merging it: the guard is silent, not loud. request() in api.ts is async, so a synchronous throw becomes a rejected promise that component-level .catch handlers swallow. The result is that unmocked boundaries stop being visible at all, trading today's ECONNREFUSED noise for silence. Worth pairing the throw with a console.error naming the URL so the output still flags it, or finding a way to fail the test outright.

Not done under ttrb-fsx9 because test/setup.ts is outside that issue's stated scope (two named test files).

Found while fixing ttrb-8zv5, by instrumenting e2e/iframe-keyboard-focus.spec.ts across 40 runs.

app/javascript/hooks/useKeyboardCommands.ts registers its document keydown listener inside a useEffect whose dependency is `handleKeyDown`, a useCallback over `commands`. Passive effects run after paint. So between the browser painting a render and React flushing that render's effects there is a window in which the DOM shows new state while the live keydown listener still closes over the previous render's values.

application.tsx:620 `handleKeyboardNext` opens with `if (entries.length === 0) return`. On boot the window lines up exactly with the moment the entry rows first paint: the rows are on screen, `entries` is populated in the newest render, and the listener still holding `entries: []` silently drops the key.

Evidence: an instrumented copy of the spec logged every /api/v1 request. On a lost press there is no `GET /api/v1/entries/<id>` at all - the handler never reached loadEntry - and a second press milliseconds later works. Rate is roughly 2 in 30 for a press issued as soon as Playwright sees the first row. Adding any latency before the press (a single page.evaluate round trip) hides it completely, which is why it only shows up under a full-suite run: 40/40 passed with instrumentation in place, 28/30 without.

User-visible form: press j in the instant the list paints and nothing happens.

Fix direction: keep the listener identity stable and read the current commands through a ref updated during render (the "latest ref" pattern), so a key arriving before effects flush still reaches this render's handlers:

  const commandsRef = useRef(commands)
  commandsRef.current = commands   // during render, read only from DOM events
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!enabled) return
    const onKeyDown = (e) => { /* read commandsRef.current */ }
    document.addEventListener("keydown", onKeyDown)
    return () => document.removeEventListener("keydown", onKeyDown)
  }, [enabled])

Updating the ref in an effect instead would reintroduce the same post-paint window.

Acceptance: a test that proves the mechanism rather than one green run - e.g. a component test that dispatches keydown synchronously after a state update that has painted but whose effects have not flushed. Note that e2e/iframe-keyboard-focus.spec.ts openFirstEntry currently retries the press to work around this; that retry should come out when this lands.
